# The committed source of requirements-dev.lock. Compile it with:
#
#     .venv/bin/python scripts/relock-dev.py
#
# which constrains every resolution to the versions the released images actually ship (agent >
# mcp > chatbot) instead of to whatever PyPI published most recently. Never run `uv pip compile`
# on this file directly: unconstrained, it is exactly what let the test environment drift two
# langchain-core minors ahead of the container the code runs in.
#
# The agent's and mcp's own direct pins, read from the pinned upstream checkout rather than
# copied, so a `COMMIT` bump in launcher/core.py moves them and a stale copy cannot linger.
# `demo.py bootstrap` creates that checkout.
-r .upstream/opentelemetry-demo/src/agent/requirements.in
-r .upstream/opentelemetry-demo/src/mcp/requirements.in

# Gradio, for the `up --debug-chatbot` client in concierge/chatbot.py. Pinned to the version the
# released chatbot image ships, for the same reason as everything above; its own transitives
# come from that image through the constraint layering. It is not in the agent or mcp images, so
# this line is what puts it in the lock at all.
#
# Not `-r` of the chatbot image's requirements.in: that file pins OpenTelemetry 1.44.0/0.65b0
# against the agent's 1.43.0/0.64b0, and no single virtualenv can hold both.
gradio==6.20.0

# Test-only tooling, in no image and therefore deliberately unpinned: these should track current
# releases, and nothing about the demo's behaviour depends on their versions.
pytest
pytest-asyncio
ruff
